-- A function may share its name with the constructor of a record that has
-- exactly one LIST field, and a warning raised inside an argument of a call
-- to it must not change which of the two the call means. The constructor
-- takes its elements as a list, so a call with two arguments is checked
-- twice, as the function and as the constructor; the call to the function
-- succeeds, with only a warning. It was once set aside for the other reading
-- and the name reported as ambiguous, when nothing said what type the call
-- has: the rules below have no GIVETH.
--
-- Each of the three rules below calls `Bag` with a CONSIDER as its first
-- argument: one misses Blue, one has a repeated arm, one is complete. The
-- first two are warned about, once each.

DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green, Blue

DECLARE Bag HAS items IS A LIST OF NUMBER

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER
      b IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE Bag a b IS a PLUS b

GIVEN c IS A Colour
DECIDE `missing case` c IS Bag (CONSIDER c WHEN Red THEN 1, WHEN Green THEN 2) 3

GIVEN c IS A Colour
DECIDE `repeated case` c IS Bag (CONSIDER c WHEN Red THEN 1, WHEN Green THEN 2, WHEN Blue THEN 2, WHEN Red THEN 2) 3

GIVEN c IS A Colour
DECIDE `complete` c IS Bag (CONSIDER c WHEN Red THEN 1, WHEN Green THEN 2, WHEN Blue THEN 2) 3

#EVAL `missing case` Red
#EVAL `repeated case` Green
#EVAL `complete` Blue
